## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests mastery of DAG dynamic programming, vertex-disjoint path packing, and careful state design to enforce disjointness while meeting exact cardinality constraints.

## Key Concepts
- DAG topological ordering enables DP by ensuring all dependencies are processed first
- Vertex-disjoint paths are guaranteed by tracking endpoint-specific states
- Path reconstruction requires storing predecessor pointers during computation
- Complexity: O(Mk + Nk) time, O(Nk) space

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode
const topo = topologicalSort(dag);
const dp = Array(N).fill(null).map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
const pred = Array(N).fill(null).map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

for (const v of topo) {
  dp[v][0] = 0;
  const bestPrev = Math.max(...dp[v]);

  for (const u of dag.inEdges(v)) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      if (dp[u][t] > -Infinity) {
        // Extend existing path
        if (dp[u][t] + val[v] > dp[v][t]) {
          dp[v][t] = dp[u][t] + val[v];
          pred[v][t] = u;
        }
        // Start new path
        if (t < k && bestPrev + val[v] > dp[v][t + 1]) {
          dp[v][t + 1] = bestPrev + val[v];
          pred[v][t + 1] = u;
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

const answer = Math.max(...dp.map(v => v[k]));
```

## Why This Question Is Asked

This problem evaluates proficiency in tree DP with disjoint-path packing and exact-path-count constraints, along with path reconstruction. It combines knapsack-on-tree techniques with path endpoint handling while guaranteeing vertex-disjointness.

## Key Concepts

- Tree DP with multiple states per node (unused vs. endpoint vs. interior)
- Knapsack-style merging across children via DP table convolution
- Path reconstruction through stored decision pointers

## Code Example

```javascript
function dfs(u, parent) {
    const dp0 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF), dp1 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF), dp2 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF);
    dp0[u][0] = dp1[u][1] = 0;
    for (const v of children[u]) {
        dfs(v, u);
        const ndp0 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF), ndp1 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF), ndp2 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF);
        for (let i = 0; i <= k; i++) for (let j = 0; j + i <= k; j++) {
            ndp0[i + j] = Math.max(ndp0[i + j], dp0[u][i] + max(dp0[v][j], dp1[v][j], dp2[v][j]));
            ndp1[i + j] = Math.max(ndp1[i + j], dp1[u][i] + max(dp0[v][j], dp2[v][j]));
            ndp2[i + j] = Math.max(ndp2[i + j], dp2[u][i] + dp0[v][j]);
        }
        dp0 = ndp0; dp1 = ndp1; dp2 = ndp2;
    }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests weighted interval scheduling with an exact k constraint and reconstruction; requires sorting, precomputing p(i), and a 2D DP with backtracking; also considerations for memory optimization.

## Key Concepts
- Weighted interval scheduling
- DP with an extra dimension for exact k selections
- Precomputing p(i) (latest non-overlapping job)
- Space-time tradeoffs; backtracking reconstruction
- Stability under large n and k; edge-case handling when K > possible non-overlapping sets

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKNonOverlapping(jobs, K) {
  // jobs: {s, e, r}; sort by end time
  jobs.sort((a,b)=> a.e - b.e);
  const n = jobs.length;
  const end = jobs.map(j=> j.e);
  // p(i): last index < i with end[p] < start[i]
  const p = new Array(n).fill(-1);
  for (let i=0; i<n; i++) {
    let lo=0, hi=i-1, ans=-1;
    while (lo<=hi){
      const mid=(lo+hi)>>1;
      if (end[mid] < jobs[i].s){ ans=mid; lo=mid+1; } else hi=mid-1;
    }
    p[i]=ans;
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(K+1).fill(0));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(K+1).fill(false));
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++){
    for (let t=1; t<=K; t++){
      const without = dp[i-1][t];
      const withTake = (p[i-1] >= 0 ? dp[p[i-1]+1][t-1] : (t-1===0?0: -Infinity)) + jobs[i-1].r;
      if (withTake > without){ dp[i][t] = withTake; take[i][t] = true; } else { dp[i][t] = without; }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction
  const res = [];
  let i=n, t=K;
  while (i>0 && t>0){
    if (take[i][t]){ res.push(i-1); i = (p[i-1] >= 0 ? p[i-1]+1 : 0); t--; }
    else { i--; }
  }
  res.reverse();
  return { revenue: dp[n][K], indices: res };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to an "at most K" constraint?
- How can you optimize memory to O(n + K) or O(K) and still reconstruct the solution?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP on partitioning with non-linear block cost, ensuring ability to precompute substring costs and reconstruct cuts.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix DP with exact k partitions
- Substring cost precomputation (distinct chars)
- Backpointers for reconstruction
- Time/space tradeoffs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Implementation outline
function maxScorePartition(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dist = Array.from({length:n}, ()=>Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let l=0;l<n;l++) {
    const seen = new Set();
    for (let r=l;r<n;r++) { seen.add(s[r]); dist[l][r] = seen.size; }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1] = dist[0][i-1];
  for (let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for (let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      for (let j=t-1;j<i;j++){
        const v = dp[j][t-1] + dist[j][i-1];
        if (v > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = v; par[i][t] = j; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted
  return {dp, par};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This problem evaluates dynamic programming expertise across string partitioning with fixed segment counts, numeric parsing constraints, and solution reconstruction—fundamental skills for real-world interview scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic programming over string prefixes and partition counts
- Handling substring numeric values with leading-zero validation
- Solution path reconstruction for determining optimal cut positions
- Time and space complexity optimization techniques

## Code Example

```javascript
function minSumPartition(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;

  // Helper to get numeric value of substring [l, r)
  const getValue = (l, r) => {
    if (s[l] === '0' && r - l > 1) return null; // leading zero
    return Number(s.slice(l, r));
  };

  const dp = Array(n + 1).fill().map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const parent = Array(n + 1).fill().map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      for (let j = t - 1; j < i; j++) {
        const val = getValue(j, i);
        if (val !== null && dp[j][t-1] + val < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = dp[j][t-1] + val;
          parent[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct cuts
  const cuts = [];
  let curr = n, currT = k;
  while (currT > 0) {
    const prev = parent[curr][currT];
    cuts.push(prev);
    curr = prev;
    currT--;
  }
  cuts.pop(); // remove 0
  cuts.reverse();

  return { minSum: dp[n][k], cuts };
}
```

## Notes

- The DP explores all possible cut positions while respecting the k-substring constraint
- Leading zero validation is crucial for correctness
- The parent array enables O(k) reconstruction of optimal cuts

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with partitioning and a nontrivial per-substring cost, plus backtracking to recover partitions.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions: dp[i][t]
- Precompute cost[l][r] in O(n^2) via two-pointer mismatches
- Backpointers for reconstruction
- Complexity: O(n^2 k) time, O(n^2) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPalindromePartitions(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const cost = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      let a = l, b = r, c = 0;
      while (a < b) { if (s[a] !== s[b]) c++; a++; b--; }
      cost[l][r] = c;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][1] = cost[0][i];
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t - 1; i < n; i++) {
      for (let p = t - 2; p < i; p++) {
        const val = dp[p][t - 1] + cost[p + 1][i];
        if (val < dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; parent[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  const splits = [];
  let i = n - 1, t = k;
  while (t >= 1) {
    splits.push(i);
    i = parent[i][t];
    t--;
  }
  splits.reverse();
  return { minCost: dp[n - 1][k], partitions: splits };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize space for large n or small k?
- How would this adapt if cost used a different per-substring metric?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP with a non-adjacent removal constraint and requires reconstruction of which indices were removed.

## Key Concepts

- 3D DP state dp[i][t][r]
- Non-adjacent constraint during removal
- Path reconstruction for the chosen indices

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxRemainingSum(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = Number.NEGATIVE_INFINITY / 4;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () =>
    Array.from({ length: k + 1 }, () => [NEG, NEG])
  );
  dp[0][0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      // keep i
      dp[i][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i-1][t][0], dp[i-1][t][1]) + A[i-1];
      // remove i
      if (t > 0) dp[i][t][1] = dp[i-1][t-1][0];
    }
  }
  return Math.max(dp[n][k][0], dp[n][k][1]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to reduce space to O(k)?
- How to modify for at-most-k removals?

## Why This Is Asked
New angle: fixed window size with non-overlap, maximizing sum of window maxima.

## Key Concepts
- Precompute windowMax to make transitions O(1).
- Prefix DP with non-overlapping constraint.
- Reconstruction via a take/parent table.

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumKWindows(A, n, k, m){
  const windowMax = Array(n+1).fill(-Infinity);
  for(let i=m; i<=n; i++){ // window ending at i
    let mx = -Infinity;
    for(let j=i-m+1; j<=i; j++) mx = Math.max(mx, A[j]);
    windowMax[i] = mx;
  }
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(false));
  for(let i=0; i<=n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1; i<=n; i++){
    for(let t=1; t<=k; t++){
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      if(i>=m && dp[i-m][t-1] + windowMax[i] > dp[i][t]){
        dp[i][t] = dp[i-m][t-1] + windowMax[i];
        take[i][t] = true;
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Combinatorial DP with partitioning; tests clear recurrence and reconstruction, common in interviews. 

## Key Concepts
- dp[i][t] = min over p of dp[p][t-1] + max(A[p..i-1]);
- Track parent to reconstruct partition points; iterate i and t in increasing order.

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1] = Math.max(...A.slice(0,i));
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      let curMax = -Infinity;
      for(let p=i-1; p>=t-1; p--){
        curMax = Math.max(curMax, A[p]);
        const cand = dp[p][t-1] + curMax;
        if(cand < dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = cand; par[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {cost: dp[n][k], par};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) time using a deque-based technique when max is a sliding property? 
- How would you extend to allow hypothetical negative numbers or to reconstruct explicit subarrays?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model multi-block dynamic programming with non-trivial block scoring that depends on range statistics (max, min) and block length. It also covers reconstruction of the exact blocks, and invites discussion of time/space trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- Partition DP with fixed k blocks
- Block score uses range (max-min) and length
- Inline maintenance of max/min while expanding the last block
- Path reconstruction with predecessor pointers

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxScore(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const parent = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      let curMax = -INF, curMin = INF;
      for (let l = i; l >= 1; l--) {
        curMax = Math.max(curMax, A[l-1]);
        curMin = Math.min(curMin, A[l-1]);
        const blockScore = (curMax - curMin) * (i - l + 1);
        if (dp[l-1][t-1] + blockScore > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = dp[l-1][t-1] + blockScore;
          parent[i][t] = l-1;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct blocks
  const blocks = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const l = parent[i][t] + 1;
    blocks.unshift([l, i]);
    i = l - 1;
    t--;
  }

  return {maxScore: dp[n][k], blocks};
}
```

## Time/Space Complexity

Time: O(n²k) - for each i and t, we scan O(n) possible l values while maintaining max/min.

Space: O(nk) for dp table plus O(nk) for parent pointers if reconstruction needed, otherwise O(nk).

## Why This Is Asked
This variation tests DP with exact count of non-overlapping segments and reconstruction, a common analytics pattern.

## Key Concepts
- Non-overlapping subarray DP
- EndHere vs global best states
- Pointer-based reconstruction
- Memory/time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k){
  const n=A.length, INF=-1e18;
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-INF));
  const end=Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-INF));
  dp[0][0]=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      end[i][t]=Math.max(end[i-1][t]+A[i-1], dp[i-1][t-1]+A[i-1]);
      dp[i][t]=Math.max(dp[i-1][t], end[i][t]);
    }
  }
  return {best: dp[n][k]};
}
```

